<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-general-solution-example" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Building the General Solution</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       RECAP BLOCK
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Time to put the last page to work. Here is the full process for building the general
    solution to a homogeneous linear system:
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="recap-general-solution-process">
    <title>Recap: Building the General Solution</title>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Have two solutions <m>\mathbf{y}</m> and <m>\mathbf{z}</m> to the homogeneous
            system (verifying them if needed).
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Compute the Wronskian <m>W</m>: the determinant of the matrix whose
            <em>columns</em> are the two solutions.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            If <m>W \neq 0</m>, the solutions are linearly independent, and the general
            solution is <m>c_1\mathbf{y} + c_2\mathbf{z}</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    The example below walks through these three steps in order.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       MAIN EXAMPLE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <example xml:id="ex-general-solution-system">
    <title>The General Solution to a System</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the system:
        <me>\mathbf{x}' = \begin{bmatrix} 9 \amp -4 \\ 14 \amp -9 \end{bmatrix} \mathbf{x}.</me>
        We are given the two candidate solutions
        <me>\mathbf{y}(t) = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix} e^{5t}
          \quad \text{and} \quad
          \mathbf{z}(t) = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 7 \end{bmatrix} e^{-5t}.</me>
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Verify that <m>\mathbf{y}</m> and <m>\mathbf{z}</m> are both solutions.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <title>Can skim or reference later if needed</title>
        <p>
          This is the same verification process as the last section: compute the derivative
          of each candidate, compute <m>\mathbf{P}</m> times each candidate, and check they
          match.
        </p>
        <p>
          For <m>\mathbf{y}(t) = \begin{bmatrix} 3e^{5t} \\ 3e^{5t} \end{bmatrix}</m>,
          differentiating gives
          <me>\frac{d\mathbf{y}}{dt} = \begin{bmatrix} 15e^{5t} \\ 15e^{5t} \end{bmatrix},</me>
          while multiplying gives
          <me>\mathbf{P}\mathbf{y}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 9 \amp -4 \\ 14 \amp -9 \end{bmatrix}
            \begin{bmatrix} 3e^{5t} \\ 3e^{5t} \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} (27 - 12)e^{5t} \\ (42 - 27)e^{5t} \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 15e^{5t} \\ 15e^{5t} \end{bmatrix}.</me>
          They match, so <m>\mathbf{y}</m> is a solution.
        </p>
        <p>
          For <m>\mathbf{z}(t) = \begin{bmatrix} 2e^{-5t} \\ 7e^{-5t} \end{bmatrix}</m>,
          differentiating gives
          <me>\frac{d\mathbf{z}}{dt} = \begin{bmatrix} -10e^{-5t} \\ -35e^{-5t} \end{bmatrix},</me>
          while multiplying gives
          <me>\mathbf{P}\mathbf{z}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 9 \amp -4 \\ 14 \amp -9 \end{bmatrix}
            \begin{bmatrix} 2e^{-5t} \\ 7e^{-5t} \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} (18 - 28)e^{-5t} \\ (28 - 63)e^{-5t} \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} -10e^{-5t} \\ -35e^{-5t} \end{bmatrix}.</me>
          They match as well, so <m>\mathbf{z}</m> is also a solution.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the Wronskian to show that <m>\mathbf{y}</m> and <m>\mathbf{z}</m> are
          linearly independent.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Place the two solutions as the columns of a matrix, then take the determinant:
          <me>W = \det\left( \begin{bmatrix} 3e^{5t} \amp 2e^{-5t} \\ 3e^{5t} \amp 7e^{-5t} \end{bmatrix} \right),
            \qquad
            \det \begin{bmatrix} a \amp b \\ c \amp d \end{bmatrix} = ad - bc.</me>
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Computing the determinant:
          <md>
            <mrow>W \amp= 3e^{5t} \cdot 7e^{-5t} - 2e^{-5t} \cdot 3e^{5t}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp= 21e^{0} - 6e^{0}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp= 21 - 6 = 15.</mrow>
          </md>
          Notice the exponentials cancel: <m>e^{5t} \cdot e^{-5t} = e^{0} = 1</m>.
          Since <m>W = 15 \neq 0</m>, the solutions <m>\mathbf{y}</m> and <m>\mathbf{z}</m>
          are linearly independent.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <conclusion>
      <p>
        Since the Wronskian is nonzero, our theorem from the last page applies: the general
        solution to the system is
        <me>\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\mathbf{y} + c_2\mathbf{z}
          = c_1 \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix} e^{5t}
          + c_2 \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 7 \end{bmatrix} e^{-5t}.</me>
        We can also combine everything into one big vector:
        <me>\mathbf{x}(t) = \begin{bmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \end{bmatrix}
          = \begin{bmatrix} 3c_1 e^{5t} + 2c_2 e^{-5t} \\ 3c_1 e^{5t} + 7c_2 e^{-5t} \end{bmatrix}.</me>
        Reading off the components, <m>x_1(t) = 3c_1 e^{5t} + 2c_2 e^{-5t}</m> and
        <m>x_2(t) = 3c_1 e^{5t} + 7c_2 e^{-5t}</m> solve the original system simultaneously,
        for any constants <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m>.
      </p>
    </conclusion>
  </example>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       NOTE: THE WRONSKIAN OFTEN INVOLVES t
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    In our example, the exponentials canceled and the Wronskian came out to a constant.
    That won't always happen: often <m>W</m> involves <m>t</m>. For example, you may find
    <me>W(\mathbf{y}, \mathbf{z}) = 12e^{-3t}.</me>
    Since <m>e^{-3t}</m> is <em>never</em> <m>0</m>, we have <m>12e^{-3t} \neq 0</m> for any
    <m>t</m>, and we can still conclude the solutions are linearly independent. A Wronskian
    that depends on <m>t</m> is perfectly fine, as long as it's never zero.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ACTIVITY: WRONSKIAN COMPUTATION (MC)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <activity xml:id="act-wronskian-computation">
    <title>Wronskian Checkpoint</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose a homogeneous linear system has the two solutions
        <me>\mathbf{y}(t) = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} e^{-t}
          \quad \text{and} \quad
          \mathbf{z}(t) = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix} e^{-2t}.</me>
        Compute the Wronskian <m>W(\mathbf{y}, \mathbf{z})</m>. Are the solutions linearly
        independent?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>W = 3e^{-3t}</m>, which is never <m>0</m>, so the solutions are linearly
            independent.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct!
            <m>W = 2e^{-t} \cdot 2e^{-2t} - 1e^{-2t} \cdot 1e^{-t} = 4e^{-3t} - e^{-3t} = 3e^{-3t}</m>.
            Even though <m>W</m> depends on <m>t</m>, the exponential <m>e^{-3t}</m> is never
            <m>0</m>, so <m>W \neq 0</m> and the solutions are linearly independent.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>W = 3e^{-3t}</m>, but since <m>W</m> depends on <m>t</m>, we cannot conclude
            the solutions are linearly independent.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The computation is right, but a <m>t</m>-dependent Wronskian is fine! The
            question is whether <m>W</m> can ever equal <m>0</m>. Since <m>e^{-3t}</m> is
            never <m>0</m>, we have <m>W = 3e^{-3t} \neq 0</m> for every <m>t</m>, and the
            solutions are linearly independent.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>W = 5e^{-3t}</m>, which is never <m>0</m>, so the solutions are linearly
            independent.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Recheck the arithmetic: the determinant is
            <m>2e^{-t} \cdot 2e^{-2t} - 1e^{-2t} \cdot 1e^{-t}</m>, which gives
            <m>4e^{-3t} - e^{-3t} = 3e^{-3t}</m>: subtract, don't add! The conclusion is
            right, though: the solutions are linearly independent.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </activity>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ACTIVITY: WRITE THE GENERAL SOLUTION (MC)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <activity xml:id="act-general-solution-vector">
    <title>General Solution Checkpoint</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Continuing the previous checkpoint: we now know
        <m>\mathbf{y}(t) = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} e^{-t}</m> and
        <m>\mathbf{z}(t) = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix} e^{-2t}</m>
        are linearly independent solutions. Which of the following is the general solution
        to the system, written as a single vector?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\mathbf{x}(t) = \begin{bmatrix} 2c_1 e^{-t} + c_2 e^{-2t} \\ c_1 e^{-t} + 2c_2 e^{-2t} \end{bmatrix}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct! The general solution is
            <m>c_1\mathbf{y} + c_2\mathbf{z}</m>. Distributing the constants and adding
            component-wise:
            <me>c_1 \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} e^{-t}
              + c_2 \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix} e^{-2t}
              = \begin{bmatrix} 2c_1 e^{-t} + c_2 e^{-2t} \\ c_1 e^{-t} + 2c_2 e^{-2t} \end{bmatrix}.</me>
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\mathbf{x}(t) = \begin{bmatrix} 2 e^{-t} + e^{-2t} \\ e^{-t} + 2 e^{-2t} \end{bmatrix}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            This is just <m>\mathbf{y} + \mathbf{z}</m>: one particular solution, not the
            general solution. The general solution needs the arbitrary constants:
            <m>c_1\mathbf{y} + c_2\mathbf{z}</m>. Without them, we only capture a single
            solution instead of all of them.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\mathbf{x}(t) = \begin{bmatrix} 2c_1 e^{-t} + 2c_2 e^{-2t} \\ c_1 e^{-t} + c_2 e^{-2t} \end{bmatrix}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Careful when combining! The components of <m>\mathbf{z}</m> are <m>1</m> (top)
            and <m>2</m> (bottom), so the <m>c_2</m> terms should be <m>c_2 e^{-2t}</m> on
            top and <m>2c_2 e^{-2t}</m> on the bottom. Here they've been mixed up.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\mathbf{x}(t) = \begin{bmatrix} 2c_1 e^{-t} + c_2 e^{-t} \\ c_1 e^{-2t} + 2c_2 e^{-2t} \end{bmatrix}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Check the exponentials: each solution keeps its own exponential in <em>both</em>
            components. The vector <m>\mathbf{y}</m> contributes <m>e^{-t}</m> to both the
            top and bottom, and <m>\mathbf{z}</m> contributes <m>e^{-2t}</m> to both. Here
            the exponentials have been split across rows, which mixes up the two solutions.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </activity>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       VIDEO + QUESTION FORM
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

